title op6: one name lost on both sides, with gains that agree

# VR9, and op6-one-move-deltas' twin.  Same shape, same rule, one
# character different in off-of, opposite verdict.
#
# Base holds one file under two names, x and y.  Both sides rename x
# and both rename it to the SAME thing:
#
#   onto    x -> p     delta: lost {x}, gained {p}
#   off-of  x -> p     delta: lost {x}, gained {p}
#
# The name x is lost on both sides and the gains agree, so this is
# the identical merge and it decides clean.  Two people made the same
# rename; there is nothing to reconcile.
#
# The rule restated on deltas has to get BOTH of these right, and it
# is easy to write one that gets only the conflict right -- a rule
# that reports whenever a name leaves both deltas would conflict
# here too, and would be wrong in the direction users notice, since
# it refuses work that agrees with itself.  That is why the pair
# exists rather than just the conflict.
#
# No antecedents are consulted in either.  The rule reads two lost
# sets and two gained sets and compares them; it never asks what
# succeeded x on either side, which is the whole point of the
# settlement.
#
# WORKED OUTCOME (by hand, before any implementation ran)
#   believed moves:  none; no directory changed
#   deltas:          onto lost {x} gained {p}; off-of lost {x} gained
#                    {p}; identical, so they merge
#   decisions:       the pool keeps dnode 10-100 and holds p and y.
#                    Realization never leaves pass one: both decided
#                    names, p and y, are names onto holds
#   conflicts:       none
#   emission:        nothing at all.  Onto already renamed x to p, the
#                    output starts as a clone of onto, and off-of
#                    asked for the same thing -- the fourth line of
#                    Section 12's table, a name that stays put in the
#                    same pool, which is the case a compiler must
#                    emit no work for

tree base
  1-1: /
    2-100: shared/
      10-100: x = v0
      10-100: y

tree onto
  1-1: /
    2-100: shared/
      10-100: p = v0
      10-100: y

tree offof
  1-1: /
    2-100: shared/
      10-100: p = v0
      10-100: y

# --- expected result, worked by hand 2026-08-31 ---
expect clean

tree expected
  1-1: /
    2-100: shared
      10-100: p = v0
      10-100: y
